<h2 id="what">What a receipt is, and what made it</h2>
<p>gVisor is a kernel written as an ordinary program: the code in the pod talks to it instead of to the machine's own kernel. Every <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#syscall">system call (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> the code makes, such as opening a file, starting a program or opening a connection, is answered by gVisor. Started with its <code>--strace</code> option, gVisor's <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#runsc">runsc (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> also writes every such call to a log file on the node (<a href="https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/debugging/">gVisor's debugging guide (EXTERNAL · official docs ↗)</a>). The node is the machine that runs the pod, and the log file is outside the pod, where the code inside cannot write. Reading package behavior from that log is also the approach of package-analysis, the Open Source Security Foundation's tool for watching what published packages do (<a href="https://github.com/ossf/package-analysis">EXTERNAL · source repo ↗</a>).</p>
<p>The <dfn>receipt</dfn> is what <code>examples/dependency-receipt</code> reads from one such log: the processes and the programs started in them, every file written, and every open for writing, file change and network call, each with its outcome. It is signed as an <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#in-toto">in-toto (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> statement (<a href="https://github.com/in-toto/attestation/blob/main/spec/v1/statement.md">the Statement specification (EXTERNAL · source repo ↗)</a>) in a <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#dsse">DSSE (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> envelope, the same envelope plimsoll signs its <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#run-record">run records (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> in. A run record is plimsoll's statement of one run: what was sent, what came back, and which sandbox ran it. The receipt names the log by its <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#digest">digest (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> and the run record by its digest, so it is about exactly one log and one run.</p>
<p>These runs used plimsoll's <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#agent-sandbox">agent-sandbox (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> provider: Kubernetes, a system that runs containers across machines, keeps a <dfn>warm pool</dfn> of ready <dfn>pods</dfn> (a pod is Kubernetes' unit of one or more containers run together; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/">Kubernetes' pods page (EXTERNAL · official docs ↗)</a>), and each run claims one, uses it once, and deletes it. The pods ran under the <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#runtimeclass">RuntimeClass (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> <code>{{.Ev.Cluster.RuntimeClass}}</code>, whose handler <code>{{.Ev.Cluster.Handler}}</code> is gVisor with the system-call log turned on; plimsolld, the plimsoll server, was told that handler is gVisor and held every pod to gVisor's boot line before sending it any code. All of it ran on a local <a href="https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/">kind (EXTERNAL · official docs ↗)</a> cluster on {{.Ev.Date}}; nothing was paid for.</p>

<h2 id="runs">The three installs</h2>
<p>Each run sent one package's files as a project and ran two steps in its pod: an offline <code>npm install</code> of the package from its directory (<code>--install-links</code> copies it into <code>node_modules</code> as an install from a tarball would, and runs its install script), then a <code>node</code> command that imports it. The packages are the fixtures beside the receipt code; none comes from the public registry, and plimsoll sends no run to a pod unless a network policy denies the pod's traffic out.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Its install script</th><th>Steps (exit code, time)</th><th>Pod UID</th><th>Tier</th><th>Run record (bundle line)</th><th>runsc log</th><th>Receipt</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{{range .Ev.Runs}}{{$r := .}}<tr>
<td><b>{{.Fixture}}</b></td>
<td>{{if .Scripts}}{{range $k, $v := .Scripts}}<code>{{$k}}: {{$v}}</code> {{end}}{{else}}none{{end}}</td>
<td>{{range $i, $s := .Steps}}{{if $i}}<br>{{end}}{{add $i 1}}: exit {{$s.Exit}}, {{sec $s.Millis}}{{end}}{{if lt (len .Steps) 2}}<br>2: not run (a step that fails stops the run){{end}}</td>
<td class="fp">{{short (uid .Instance) 8}}…</td>
<td>{{.Isolation}}</td>
<td class="fp">{{short .Record 12}} ({{.Line}})</td>
