<p class="small">Tier is the <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#isolation-tier">isolation tier (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a> the run record states: <code>kernel</code> means gVisor stood between the code and the node's kernel. Every request asked for at least <code>{{.Quiet.Floor}}</code>, and the daemon was set to refuse any run below it. Each run record's start and end are {{sec .Quiet.Millis}} (quiet), {{sec .Network.Millis}} (network) and {{sec .Canary.Millis}} (canary) apart. The log sizes are as runsc wrote them; the published copies are gzip-compressed.</p>
<p>What each package's install script does, from its source in <code>examples/dependency-receipt/fixtures/packages</code>: <b>quiet</b> has none. <b>network</b> starts a TCP connection (the kind a web request uses) to 203.0.113.7 port 443, looks up the name <code>canary.example</code> (a DNS lookup, which turns a name into an address), and sends a UDP datagram (one packet, sent with no connection) to 198.51.100.9 port 53. Both addresses are reserved for documentation, so no real host has them (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5737">RFC 5737 (EXTERNAL · standard ↗)</a>). <b>canary</b> first writes <code>/root/.canary</code>; if that works it goes on to write through a symlink into <code>/etc</code>, rename and remove a file, and write to <code>/proc</code>. Here its first write was refused, so its script stopped and npm failed:</p>
{{range .Canary.Steps}}{{if ne .Exit 0}}<details><summary>What npm printed for the canary install (exit {{.Exit}})</summary><pre>$ {{.Command}}
{{.Stdout}}{{.Stderr}}</pre></details>{{end}}{{end}}

<h2 id="who">Who did what in each pod</h2>
<p>A receipt covers the pod's whole life, not only the package: sandboxd (agent-sandbox's daemon in the pod, which starts each command plimsoll sends), plimsoll's own check of the pod, plimsoll's runner, npm, and the package. The receipt lists every process by its PID (process ID), the programs started in it, and which process made each call. This page groups the processes by the command that started them: a step's shell is matched to the step's exact text in the signed request, and an install script's shell to the script's exact text in the <code>package.json</code> the run sent. The grouping is the page's reading; the receipt itself lists every process and call ungrouped.</p>
<p><span class="chip eff">effected</span> the call returned success, <span class="chip att">attempted</span> it returned an error, <span class="chip und">undetermined</span> the log does not show its outcome (the next section says more), <span class="chip wr">written</span> files written. For the package's own groups (amber) every call is listed; for the others, every call that did not succeed and every network call other than opening a new socket.</p>
{{range .Ev.Runs}}{{$r := .}}
