</div>
{{end}}
<p class="aside">Most of what the receipts list is npm, not the package. npm turns on node's compile cache (<a href="https://nodejs.org/api/module.html#module-compile-cache">node's module documentation (EXTERNAL · official docs ↗)</a>), and in the quiet run {{range .Quiet.Seen.Groups}}{{if and (eq .Kind "step") (eq .Step 1)}}{{comma .CompileCache}} of the {{comma .Written}} files npm wrote{{end}}{{end}} are that cache. A package's own groups are short by comparison: that is what grouping by process is for.</p>

<h2 id="sides">The receipt's three sides, in plain words</h2>
<p>Every open for writing, file change and network call in a receipt sits on exactly one of three sides, decided by what the call returned in the log.</p>
<div class="sides">
<div class="eff"><h3>Effected</h3><p>The call returned success: it did what it asked. The network package's script sent {{.NetSends}} {{plural .NetSends "datagram" "datagrams"}} to port 53 of 127.0.0.1, the address the pod's name resolver is set to. Its source looks up <code>canary.example</code>, and a lookup shows in the log as a datagram to port 53, never as the name.</p></div>
<div class="att"><h3>Attempted</h3><p>The call returned an error. The canary script's open of <code>{{.Refused.What}}</code> answered <code>{{.Refused.Result}}</code>, the error number and name the call returned: the code runs as user 61000 on a read-only root. Of the {{.Attempted}} attempted calls in the three receipts, {{.AttemptedMkdir}} are a <code>mkdir</code> answering that the directory was already there (file exists) or that its parent was not there yet, and {{.AttemptedNscd}} are a connect to <code>/var/run/nscd/socket</code>, a name-service socket that does not exist in the pod. Attempted means the call failed, not that a policy stopped it, and never that the package did not try.</p></div>
<div class="und"><h3>Undetermined</h3><p>The log does not show the outcome. The network script's <code>connect</code> to {{.Connect.What}} answered <code>{{.Connect.Result}}</code>: node connects without waiting, and whether the connection ever completed is decided by calls the log does not follow. plimsoll refuses a pod unless a network policy denies its traffic out{{if .EgressBlocked}} and this daemon's startup check saw all {{.EgressTried}} outbound connections it tried from the pool's pods time out or find no route{{end}}. The receipt does not lean on any of that: it says only that the connection was started.</p></div>
</div>

<h2 id="join">Which run it was</h2>
<p>plimsoll reads each claimed pod back from Kubernetes before it sends any code, and the run record names the pod by its UID, the unique ID the API server gave it when it was made. Kubernetes never gives that UID to another object in the cluster's history. runsc logs each container's spec when the pod starts, before any program in it runs, and the spec names the same UID. The receipt command verifies the run record's signature in the <dfn>bundle</dfn> (the <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#harness">harness (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a>'s signed file of run records, one per line), requires the two UIDs to be equal, and names the record by its digest as the receipt's second subject.</p>
{{range .Ev.Runs}}<div class="join">
<div><b>Run record</b>, bundle line {{.Line}}<br><span class="fp">instance k8s-pod-uid:{{uid .Instance}}<br>record {{.Record}}</span></div>
<div class="eq">=</div>
<div><b>runsc log</b> of the {{.Fixture}} run<br><span class="fp">container spec, pod UID {{uid .Log.Instance}}<br>log {{.Log.SHA256}}</span></div>
</div>{{end}}
<div class="case">
<p><b>The wrong run's record is refused.</b> Signing the network run's log joined to the quiet run's record (bundle line {{.Quiet.Line}}). This and the commands below ran in the live run's scratch directory, where the receipts were not yet compressed and the key that signed them sat in <code>secrets/</code>:</p>
{{range .Ev.Transcripts}}{{if eq .Name "sign the network log joined to the quiet run's record"}}<pre>$ dependency-receipt {{range .Args}}{{.}} {{end}}
{{.Stderr}}exit {{.Exit}}</pre>{{end}}{{end}}
<p class="verdict">Refused before anything was signed.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="tamper">Changing a receipt or its log fails the check</h2>
<p>The check opens a receipt under the signer's public key, rebuilds the whole claim from the log and the run record it names, and requires the two to be identical, byte for byte. Each receipt above checked. Two changed inputs, on the network run:</p>
<div class="case">
<p><b>A connection deleted from the receipt.</b> The receipt opened, the call <code>connect {{.Ev.Dropped.Call.Addr}} port {{.Ev.Dropped.Call.Port}}</code> removed from its <i>{{.Ev.Dropped.Side}}</i> side, and the rest signed again with the same key: a signer who leaves a call out. The signature is good; the log still shows the call.</p>
{{range .Ev.Transcripts}}{{if eq .Name "check a receipt with a connection deleted"}}<pre>$ dependency-receipt {{range .Args}}{{.}} {{end}}
{{.Stderr}}exit {{.Exit}}</pre>{{end}}{{end}}
<p class="verdict">Refused: the log shows something other than the receipt states.</p>
</div>
<div class="case">
<p><b>One byte of the log changed.</b> Byte {{comma .Edit.Offset}} of the {{comma .Network.Log.Bytes}}-byte log, the last digit of the address the first time the log names it, changed from {{.Edit.Was}} to {{.Edit.Now}}, so the log would say the package connected somewhere else:</p>
<p class="edit">…{{.Edit.Before}}<mark>{{.Edit.Was}}→{{.Edit.Now}}</mark>{{.Edit.After}}…</p>
{{range .Ev.Transcripts}}{{if eq .Name "check the receipt against the log with one byte changed"}}<pre>$ dependency-receipt {{range .Args}}{{.}} {{end}}
{{.Stderr}}exit {{.Exit}}</pre>{{end}}{{end}}
<p class="verdict">Refused: the receipt is about another log.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="limits">What the log cannot show</h2>
<p>A receipt is exactly as good as the log under it. These are its limits, stated in the receipt code's README; nothing on this page gets around them.</p>
<ul class="limits">
<li><b>The log is the signer's word.</b> Whoever runs the node holds the log. The check proves the receipt says exactly what this log shows and names this run; it cannot prove runsc wrote this log for that pod.</li>
<li><b>Behavior the package keeps for later.</b> One install and one import is one observation. Code that waits, or checks whether it is watched, shows nothing.</li>
<li><b>Names looked up in DNS</b> show as datagrams to port 53, not the names.</li>
<li><b>The destination of a datagram sent with <code>sendmsg</code></b>, which node's UDP sockets use: the network script's datagram to 198.51.100.9 is in its receipt {{if eq .NetHidden 1}}as one send{{else}}as {{.NetHidden}} sends{{end}} with no destination.</li>
<li><b>Writes through shared memory mappings</b>, <code>sendfile</code>, <code>splice</code>, <code>copy_file_range</code> and <code>fallocate</code> are outside the calls the log keeps, so an open for writing can appear with no write after it.</li>
<li><b>Paths of calls that take no descriptor</b> (<code>mkdir</code>, <code>rename</code>, <code>unlink</code>, <code>open</code>) are read from the program's memory before the call reads them, so a second thread can change them in between, and a symlink in a folder of the path can make the call land elsewhere. Files written are named from descriptors and are not affected.</li>
<li><b>Starting a new program</b> can close descriptors silently, so after one the reader leaves a descriptor's name unresolved rather than give it a new file's name.</li>
<li><b>io_uring</b>, Linux's interface for queued file and network work, which runsc leaves off unless started with <code>--iouring</code>; the receipt is refused for a log made with it on.</li>
<li><b>Anything outside gVisor.</b> plimsoll's providers that run each sandbox in a small virtual machine of its own (Kata Containers, E2B, Docker Cloud) have no such log, so no receipt.</li>
<li><b>The whole pod is one observation.</b> sandboxd, plimsoll's check, the runner and npm are in every receipt with the package; this page's grouping is a reading of the process tree, not part of the signed claim.</li>
<li><b>This cluster is a local test setup.</b> The pod network between plimsolld and sandboxd is kind's default, which is not encrypted, so these runs show the receipt, not a protected channel (<a href="https://github.com/plimsollmark/plimsoll/blob/main/docs/agentsandbox.md">plimsoll's agent-sandbox guide (EXTERNAL · source repo ↗)</a>). The daemon was not in <a href="../../trainers/glossary.html#hardened-mode">hardened mode (INTERNAL · glossary →)</a>, and the image is named by tag; each run's record states the image's digest as the node reported it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every receipt also signs this list of what it cannot see, so its limits travel with it:</p>
<ul class="limits small">{{range .Ev.NotObserved}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}</ul>

